<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Coup of 18 Brumaire</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1799">1799</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Coup of 18 Brumaire was the seizure of power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a> on 9–10 November 1799 (18–19 Brumaire, Year VIII, in the revolutionary calendar). With the help of the director Emmanuel-Joseph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emmanuel-joseph-sieyes">Sieyès</a> and of troops who dispersed the legislative councils, Bonaparte overthrew the Directory and set up the Consulate, with himself as First <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">Consul</a>. It is usually taken as the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/coup-of-18-brumaire.jpg" alt="Coup of 18 Brumaire" width="320" height="341" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: François Bouchot · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bouchot_-_Le_general_Bonaparte_au_Conseil_des_Cinq-Cents.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Napoleon Bonaparte seized power in the coup of <b>18 Brumaire</b> in 1799 and governed as First Consul, restoring order through the Concordat of 1801 and the Civil Code.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-first-republic">French First Republic</a></li></ul></section>
